{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "06", "section": "06", "chapter_title": "GitHub Pages Personal Website", "title": "Portfolio Evidence and Reflection", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting portfolio evidence and reflection to resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website", "cat100_supplement_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 6, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/06/05"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_6_6_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_6_6_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_6_6_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_6_6_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection_calibration"]}, "content": "# Portfolio Evidence and Reflection\n\n![Portfolio Evidence and Reflection textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/06-06-portfolio-evidence-and-reflection.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA portfolio and a resume both make claims about what you can do, and a claim is only as strong as the evidence behind it. A resume bullet that says \"responsible for spreadsheets\" claims a duty; one that says \"built a 3-tab budget tracker that cut reconciliation from two hours to twenty minutes\" claims a result a reader can picture and a hiring manager can ask about. The difference is the move at the center of this section: turning vague responsibility language into specific, truthful, measurable evidence aimed at one role.\n\nTwo hazards make this harder than it looks. The first is inflation: AI rewriters love to exaggerate (\"led a national team of twelve\") in ways that are not true and that collapse in an interview. The second is mismatch: a strong bullet aimed at the wrong role still misses. This section teaches you to write evidence that is concrete, honest, role-fit, and accompanied by an AI-use note that keeps the revision auditable.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student applies to a data-analyst posting that asks for Excel and reporting. Her resume bullet reads \"Responsible for club spreadsheets and various data tasks.\" It is technically true and completely forgettable: no artifact, no skill named, no result, nothing the reviewer can verify or ask about. She runs it through an AI rewriter, which returns \"Led a national finance team of twelve, driving organizational strategy.\" It sounds impressive and it is a lie; she led no national team. If she submits it, the first interview question (\"tell me about that national team\") will expose it.\n\nThe weak bullet fails by saying too little; the AI bullet fails by saying something false. The skill this section teaches is the narrow, defensible path between them: a bullet that is specific, measurable, and true.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nStrong portfolio evidence follows a learnable structure.\n\n**Action plus artifact plus result.** A duty statement (\"responsible for X\") names a role. An evidence statement names what you built (the artifact), how (the action and tools), and what changed (the result, ideally measured). \"Built a 3-tab Excel budget tracker using VLOOKUP that cut monthly reconciliation from about two hours to about twenty minutes\" has all three. The measured result is what makes it concrete; even an approximate number (\"about twenty minutes\") beats no number.\n\n**Role fit.** Evidence is aimed, not generic. Before writing, read the posting and name the skills it asks for, then surface the experiences that match. For a posting listing Excel and reporting, the budget-tracker bullet is on target; a bullet about your soccer team, however true, is not. The same underlying experience can be framed toward different roles by foregrounding different parts of it.\n\n**Truthfulness and the AI-use note.** Every claim must be defensible in an interview, which rules out inflation. When AI helps with wording, the honest move is to keep the facts yours and add a short note: \"AI assisted with wording and verb choice only; all numbers and claims are mine and verifiable.\" The note is not a confession; it is an audit trail that signals integrity.\n\nThese ideas are NACE career readiness made literal: the competency framework prizes communication and ethical judgment, and a truthful, role-fit, evidence-based bullet demonstrates both. ISTE digital citizenship adds the responsible-AI dimension: using a rewriter is fine; passing off its fabrications as fact is not.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An AI rewriter turns your true club bullet into Led a national finance team of twelve, but you led no national team. What is the right move?\" explanation=\"Impressiveness that is not true is a liability, because resumes are interrogated. The inflated line would collapse at the first follow-up question (tell me about that national team). Delete the fabrication, keep the true measurable result, and add a one-line AI-use note so the facts stay visibly yours.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Delete it: a false claim collapses in the interview, so keep the true measurable bullet instead\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Keep it: a more impressive-sounding bullet raises your odds of an interview\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Keep it but soften twelve to several so it feels less specific\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nMaria is revising one resume bullet for a data-analyst posting that lists Excel and reporting. Watch the before-and-after with evidence, role fit, and an AI-use note.\n\n**Step 1. The weak before.**\n\n```text\n- Responsible for club spreadsheets and various data tasks.\n```\n\nThis is a duty statement: no artifact, no skill, no result, and nothing a reader can verify.\n\n**Step 2. Gather the real evidence.** What did she actually build? A 3-tab budget tracker using VLOOKUP that replaced a manual process. Measured result: monthly reconciliation went from about 2 hours to about 20 minutes.\n\n**Step 3. The strong after, tailored to the role.**\n\n```text\n- Built a 3-tab Excel budget tracker (VLOOKUP, pivot summary) for a 60-member\n  student club, cutting monthly reconciliation from ~2 hours to ~20 minutes.\n```\n\n**Step 4. Apply the truthfulness and AI-use checks.** An AI rewrite had suggested \"Led a national finance team of 12.\" Maria deletes it because it never happened. She adds a one-line note: \"AI assisted with wording and verb choice only; all numbers and claims are mine and verifiable.\"\n\nThe after bullet names a concrete artifact, a real measurable result, and the skills the posting asks for. The fabricated AI claim is removed, and the AI-use note keeps the revision honest and auditable.\n\nOrder the moves that turn a vague duty statement into a defensible, role-fit bullet. Gather the real evidence before you write, and attach the AI-use note last.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Read the posting and name the skills it actually asks for\",\"Recall the real artifact you built and the measurable result it produced\",\"Rewrite the bullet as action plus artifact plus measured result\",\"Run the truth check and delete any claim you could not defend in an interview\",\"Run the fit check so the bullet foregrounds the posting skills\",\"Attach a one-line AI-use note keeping the numbers and claims yours\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore a resume bullet ships, hold it to three tests:\n\n1. **Specificity check:** Does it name an artifact, an action, and a result? If it says only \"responsible for,\" rewrite it around what you actually built and what changed.\n2. **Truth check:** Could you defend every word in an interview? If a claim would crumble under \"tell me more,\" delete it, no matter how good it sounds.\n3. **Fit check:** Does it foreground the skills this specific posting asks for? A true bullet aimed at the wrong role still misses.\n\nA bullet that passes all three is ready. If AI touched the wording, attach the AI-use note so the facts remain visibly yours.\n\nWalk the bullet Responsible for club spreadsheets and various data tasks through the three tests and predict each verdict before you reveal it. The target posting lists Excel and reporting.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Test the weak bullet with the specificity, truth, and fit checks\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Specificity check first: does the bullet name an artifact, an action, and a result?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, responsible for spreadsheets names the work clearly\",\"No, it states a duty only: no artifact, no tool, no measurable result\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Responsible for is a duty statement. There is no artifact, no named tool, and no result a reader can picture or verify, so it fails the specificity check.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Fit check next: does it foreground the Excel and reporting skills the posting lists?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, spreadsheets implies Excel and reporting strongly enough\",\"No, it is too generic to surface the specific skills the posting wants\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Various data tasks hides rather than foregrounds the relevant skills. The rewrite should name Excel, VLOOKUP, and the reporting result so the fit is obvious.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Verdict: rewrite around the real artifact, for example Built a 3-tab Excel budget tracker using VLOOKUP that cut monthly reconciliation from about two hours to about twenty minutes. That passes specificity and fit; the truth check then guards against inflating the numbers.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most dangerous misreading is that a more impressive-sounding bullet is a better bullet. Impressiveness that is not true is a liability, because resumes are interrogated, not just read. The inflated \"national team\" line does not raise your odds; it sets a trap for the interview.\n\nA second misreading is thinking using AI on a resume is dishonest in itself. It is not. Using AI to polish wording is ordinary; the line is crossed only when the model invents facts and you let them stand. The AI-use note is what keeps you on the right side of that line.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the rule to the budget-tracker bullet. The before reads \"Responsible for club spreadsheets and various data tasks\" and fails the specificity check on every count: no artifact, no action, no result. The student gathers the real evidence: she built a 3-tab tracker using VLOOKUP, and it cut monthly reconciliation from about two hours to about twenty minutes. She rewrites the after to name the artifact, the tools, the scale (a 60-member club), and the measured time saved, passing the specificity check.\n\nShe then runs the truth check on the AI rewrite that proposed \"Led a national finance team of twelve\" and deletes it, because it never happened and would not survive a single follow-up question. She runs the fit check against the posting (Excel, reporting) and confirms the tracker bullet is squarely on target. Finally she adds the AI-use note: \"AI assisted with wording and verb choice only; all numbers and claims are mine and verifiable.\" The result is a bullet that is concrete, defensible, aimed, and auditable.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader can spot which test a weak bullet fails and name the fix. As you study the worked example, take each version of the bullet and label the failing check (specificity, truth, or fit) before reading the explanation. If your diagnosis matches, you can revise your own bullets without waiting for feedback.\n\nA strong reader of portfolio evidence can answer four questions about any bullet:\n\n1. What artifact, action, and result does it name, and which is missing?\n2. Which claim would you have to defend in an interview, and could you?\n3. Does it foreground the skills the target posting actually lists?\n4. If AI shaped the wording, is there a note keeping the facts clearly yours?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour before-after is strong when a skeptical reviewer reading the after bullet would have nothing to doubt: the artifact is named, the number is plausible and yours, and the framing matches the role. The reflection should show not just that you improved the bullet but that you removed a tempting falsehood and can say why.\n\nA strong submission names the inflation you rejected: \"I deleted the AI-suggested national-team line because it was false, and a false claim is worse than a modest true one because the interview would expose it.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same evidence discipline to a different artifact: a LinkedIn summary or a graduate-school statement instead of a resume bullet. The format and length change, but the three tests do not. A LinkedIn summary still needs specific results over vague duties, still must be defensible, and still must be aimed at the audience you want (recruiters in a particular field). The AI-use judgment carries over too: polishing prose is fine, inventing accomplishments is not. The same true budget-tracker story can anchor a resume bullet, a LinkedIn line, and an interview answer, framed slightly differently each time. Reusing one honest piece of evidence across formats, without inflating it, is the sign you learned evidence discipline rather than one bullet.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/06-06-portfolio-evidence-and-reflection-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"06.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note for CAT 100 06.06. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection\", \"cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website\", \"evidence_based_revision\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection", "cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website"], "stem": "Which resume bullet gives a reader the strongest, most verifiable evidence of impact?", "options": ["Responsible for spreadsheets", "Built a 3-tab Excel budget tracker with VLOOKUP that cut the club's monthly reconciliation time from 2 hours to 20 minutes", "Hard worker who is good with computers", "Did various data tasks as needed"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A strong bullet names the artifact, the skill, and a measurable result a reader can picture and verify. Vague duty statements carry no evidence.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_duties_count_as_evidence", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection"], "stem": "You took one short tutorial in Python. Which resume phrasing is both truthful and useful?", "options": ["Expert Python developer", "Completed an introductory Python course; wrote a 40-line script that cleaned a 500-row CSV", "Mastered all of Python", "Python since birth"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Truthful framing states the actual scope (intro course, a concrete script) so a reader is not misled. Overclaiming expertise fails the first technical question.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_overclaiming_skills", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat100_supplement_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection"], "stem": "An AI tool rewrites your resume and invents a bullet: 'Led a team of 12 to launch a national campaign.' You never did this. What is the right action?", "options": ["Keep it; it sounds impressive and AI wrote it", "Delete the fabricated bullet and keep only claims you can back with real evidence; note that AI was used for wording only", "Keep it but lower the number to 6", "Submit it and explain later if asked"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "AI will happily fabricate accomplishments. You stay accountable for truthfulness: remove invented claims and disclose that AI helped with wording, not facts.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_ai_fabrication_accepted", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_portfolio_evidence_and_reflection", "evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "You are tailoring one resume for a data-analyst posting that lists Excel and visualization. Which revision shows role fit?", "options": ["Lead with an unrelated babysitting bullet because it came first chronologically", "Move the Excel dashboard and chart-design bullets to the top and trim bullets unrelated to data work", "Add every job you ever had in full detail to look experienced", "Use a rainbow color scheme to stand out"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Role fit means surfacing the evidence the specific posting asks for. Promoting the relevant bullets and trimming the rest signals fit better than length or decoration.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_one_resume_fits_all", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_duties_count_as_evidence", "pattern": "duties_mistaken_for_impact", "description": "Writing resume bullets that list responsibilities or vague traits instead of a concrete artifact and a measurable result", "trigger": "learner writes Responsible for spreadsheets or Hard worker as evidence", "feedback": "Replace duty statements with artifact plus result: what you built, the skill it shows, and a number or outcome a reader can picture.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_overclaiming_skills", "pattern": "overclaiming_skill_level", "description": "Inflating skill level on a resume (expert, mastered) beyond what the actual experience supports", "trigger": "learner labels an introductory experience as expert or mastery", "feedback": "State the true scope: the course you took and the concrete thing you made. Honest framing survives the first interview question; inflation does not.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_ai_fabrication_accepted", "pattern": "ai_fabricated_accomplishment_kept", "description": "Keeping an AI-invented accomplishment on a resume because it sounds impressive, even though it never happened", "trigger": "learner retains a fabricated bullet generated by an AI rewrite", "feedback": "You are accountable for every claim. Delete anything you cannot back with real evidence, and disclose that AI helped with wording, not facts.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_6_6_one_resume_fits_all", "pattern": "single_untailored_resume", "description": "Submitting one untailored resume for every posting instead of surfacing the evidence the specific role asks for", "trigger": "learner leaves unrelated bullets at the top and ignores the posting's named skills", "feedback": "Tailor to the posting: promote the bullets that match its required skills and trim the rest. Role fit beats length or decoration.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}